   "part": "Drifting apart",
   "text": "In 2008, Ukraine's leaders asked to start the process of joining NATO. At Bucharest, NATO said Ukraine and Georgia \"will become members of NATO\". It gave no date and agreed to further talks. Neither country received a Membership Action Plan at the summit. A Russian newspaper, Kommersant, later reported from an unnamed source that, in a closed meeting, President Putin told President Bush: \"You understand, George, that Ukraine isn't even a state.\" We have no verified transcript for those words. After months of rising tensions, Georgian forces shelled Tskhinvali, in South Ossetia, on the night of the seventh to the eighth of August. Russian forces then advanced deep into Georgia. Later that month, Russia recognised South Ossetia and Abkhazia as independent. In Ukraine, opinion on NATO was divided. In a survey in 2009, more people opposed joining than supported it."
